oked like slowed down by  today's standards these tiles would be a  type constraint but developers managed  to make racecars aliens spaceships and  robots these games look the way they did  because they were exercised as an  efficiency and nothing illustrates that  precarious balance of conserving  computer memory more than the letters  and numbers that guided players through  the game sprint twos set of characters  are sort of like the arcade fonts  earliest fossils and they can be traced  back to another game from 1976 these  characters like most arcade fonts or  mono spaced as the name suggests the  same width and because every tile on the  screen is locked together that 8x8 grid  also has to account for the gap between  characters the quiz show character set  stands out first and foremost because of  its proportions because with those rules  I would probably draw 1/8 like this but  the designers of this font also  attempted to follow the calligraphy  tradition of typography ok this is the  quiz show 8 that stresses also how they  differentiated the zero from the O so  the quiz font was designed in the US and  variations of it showed up in a lot of  Atari games but it didn't truly travel  the globe until it jumped over to game  developers in Japan often weren't  familiar with the Latin alphabet  making their own games they just useless  I thought increase your house  there's Japanese developers still  couldn't resist putting their own spin  on it by adding and subtracting a few  pixels here and there you might  recognize this modified W and Y from the  hit game and Galaxian these typeface  variations also had their weird quirks  especially when lowercase letters  entered the picture the biggest  challenge was figuring out exactly what  lowercase letters were supposed to look  like case in point the lowercase  characters for the Japanese made game  rock and rope-tow she found them saved  within the code of the game but they  weren't actually used probably for the 